text:
All three modes are now functional. You can test by editing your config.

To flip a topic to a different mode, add to .instar/config.json:

  "topicFrameworks": {
    "9984": "claude-code",
    "9985": "claude-code-agent-sdk",
    "9986": "codex-cli"
  }

What each mode does:
- claude-code: your normal Claude session, OAuth subscription billing (current default behavior).
- claude-code-agent-sdk: same Claude binary, but API-key auth so it bills against the separate $200/mo Max 20x credit bucket from Anthropic's June notice.
- codex-cli: Codex CLI with safe-but-agentic sandbox.

For the agent-sdk mode you'll need to drop an API key into config:

  "sessions": {
    "credentials": {
      "anthropic-agent-sdk": { "kind": "api-key", "value": "sk-ant-api03-..." }
    }
  }

I went with interpretation (i) of your mode (a) question — same Claude binary, separate billing — since you didn't reply in time and that interpretation matches your June 2026 credit notice context. If you actually wanted (ii) the programmatic Agent SDK package, revert is clean.

What I'd want to verify before declaring this done: actually spawn one session per mode in real Telegram topics and make sure the agent responds. The fake-binary tests prove the spawn shape is right but they can't prove auth works against real Anthropic/OpenAI endpoints. Try it on a throwaway topic and let me know if anything breaks.

Two known limitations to note: Codex sessions don't fire your Claude-specific hooks (compaction recovery, etc.) — that's a v1.0.0 known gap. And Codex resume isn't wired (it's a CLI subcommand rather than a flag) so a respawned Codex topic starts fresh.
